The implementer stopped where I told it to, and its finding is correct. Removing a queued prompt (`RemoveQueuedPrompt` or `ClearQueuedPrompts`) produces no outcome. Today the only thing that answers such a prompt is the reminder rule: a reminder turn counts as answering everything accepted before the reminded turn finished. Copying the previous value alone would break that.

There is a version of option A that covers this with no special cases. The projection records one fact at the moment it records any outcome, using its own state at that event: every prompt accepted before this outcome is answered unless it is still queued. That makes `answered_through = min(completed ordinal, earliest still-queued ordinal − 1)`, and never lower than the previous outcome's value.

That single rule covers:
- prompts that are removed;
- prompts that are steered into a turn;
- reminder turns, which reproduces the old reminder rule exactly;
- harness turns.

The readers still read only the field. The rule is computed once, by the one owner, from one consistent state. That is different from the reader-side inference that kept failing review, which combined several separate reads. I'll send this to the same implementer.
